Concept ACK because making trace logging more accessible is a good goal, but approach NACK because having three options (`-debug`, `-trace`, `-debugexclude`) interact to control log levels is unnecessarily confusing when a single option should cover it.

The interactions aren't obvious from the names: `-trace=net` also enables debug logging, `-debugexclude=net` disables trace even though the name only mentions debug, and when both `-debug` and `-trace` apply to the same category, trace wins and the debug setting is ignored. That last point makes some reasonable configurations impossible: you can't set trace as the default log level while keeping specific categories at debug, because any per-category `-debug=<cat>` will be overridden by `-trace=all`. In most logging frameworks this is just `-loglevel=trace,net:debug`.

I posted a more detailed analysis in #30486 and implemented an alternative in #35387 that handles all of this with a single option and is fully backwards compatible. If #35387 has design problems of its own, I'm interested to hear them and would withdraw this nack.
